---
import { rockJsonLd } from '../../../data/rockJsonLd';
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import ProjectHero from '../../../components/ProjectHero.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const IMG = (p: string) => `/images/wp/${p}`;

interface KCard {
  title: string;
  image: string;
  alt: string;
  desc: string;
  href: string;
}

const koreanWar: KCard[] = [
  { title: 'Seina', image: `https://media.cabin1701.com/japoneson/rock/voices/voice-seina.jpg`, alt: `Portrait of Shoko "Seina" Shiraishi`, desc: 'From Kitakyushu to New York and Havana — Why a Jazz Singer Preserves Mentai Rock', href: `${base}en/rock/voices/#seina` },
  { title: 'Dobb', image: `https://media.cabin1701.com/japoneson/rock/voices/voice-dobb.jpg`, alt: `Portrait of Dobb, Kitakyushu rock historian and musician`, desc: '“If you want to know the history of Kitakyushu rock, ask him.”', href: `${base}en/rock/voices/#dobb` },
  { title: 'Juntaro Fukuda', image: `https://media.cabin1701.com/japoneson/rock/voices/voice-juntaro.jpg`, alt: `Portrait of Juntaro Fukuda, pioneer of Kitakyushu rock`, desc: '“Fukuoka rock begins with Juntaro.”', href: `${base}en/rock/voices/#juntaro` },
  { title: 'Keiichi Takano', image: `https://media.cabin1701.com/japoneson/rock/takano/takano.jpg`, alt: `Portrait of Keiichi Takano`, desc: '"The Roosters Shone Like No Other"', href: `${base}en/rock/takano/` },
];

const trail = [
  { slug: 'rock', title: 'MENTAI ROCK', path: `${base}en/rock/` },
];
---

<Layout title="Mentai Rock — Japonéson" description={`"Mentai Rock," the rock of Fukuoka and Kitakyushu, heard through the smoke and steel of Japan's industrial heartland: Sheena & The Rokkets, The Roosters, and eyewitness voices.`} lang="en" jsonLd={rockJsonLd({ lang: "en", path: "/en/rock/", title: 'Mentai Rock — Japonéson', description: `"Mentai Rock," the rock of Fukuoka and Kitakyushu, heard through the smoke and steel of Japan's industrial heartland: Sheena & The Rokkets, The Roosters, and eyewitness voices.` })} ogImage="images/rock/hero.jpg">
  <Header lang="en" />
  <main class="rock-main">
    <ProjectHero
      title="Mentai Rock"
      subtitle={"The Industrial Blues:\nDetroit and Northern Kyushu"}
      titleFont="var(--font-display-cinzel)"
      titleWeight={700}
      titleSize="60px"
      titleColor="#e02a46"
      image={`${base}images/rock/hero.jpg`}
      imageAlt={`Sheena and Makoto Ayukawa of Sheena & The Rokkets performing live on stage. Photo by FU.`}
      align="center"
      height={1000}
      fit="cover"
      overlayTop={0}
      overlayBottom={0}
    />

    <div class="container breadcrumb-wrap">
      <Breadcrumb trail={trail} lang="en" />
    </div>

    <section class="intro">
      <div class="intro-inner">
        <div class="motto">
          <p class="motto-ja">「福岡は日本のリバプールやけんね！やっぱ、めんたいやらんといけんやろ！」</p>
          <p class="motto-tr">"Fukuoka is the Liverpool of Japan, you know—so we've gotta keep Mentai alive!"</p>
        </div>

        <hr class="navy-rule" />

        <h2 class="section-q">What Is "Mentai Rock"?</h2>
        <p class="sub-q"><em>Sharp, spicy, and unsweetened—gritty, beat-driven rock 'n' roll</em></p>

        <hr class="navy-rule" />

        <div class="rock-maps">
          <img src={`https://media.cabin1701.com/japoneson/rock/map-fukuoka-en.svg`} alt="Map of Fukuoka Prefecture, Japan, showing Fukuoka City and Kitakyushu." loading="lazy" />
          <img src={`https://media.cabin1701.com/japoneson/rock/map-england-en.svg`} alt="Map of North West England, UK, showing Liverpool and Manchester." loading="lazy" />
        </div>

        <p>
          "Mentai Rock" was heavily influenced by the beats of the 1960s British Invasion.
        </p>

        <p>
          Historically, Hakata (Fukuoka City) has often been compared to Liverpool—the birthplace of The Beatles, Elvis Costello, Echo & the Bunnymen, and Badfinger—while Kitakyushu has been likened to the industrial city of Manchester, home to New Order, The Smiths, The Stone Roses, and Oasis.
        </p>

        <p>
          Broadly speaking, both belong to the same "Fukuoka Prefecture," yet Fukuoka City and Kitakyushu City possess completely different characters. In terms of the Kanto region around Tokyo, they differ in image, temperament, and economic backbone just like Yokohama and Kawasaki. Fukuoka City corresponds to Yokohama—a bustling commercial hub—whereas Kitakyushu corresponds to Kawasaki, thriving as a heavy industrial zone and a working-class, blue-collar town.
        </p>

        <p>
          The distinct backgrounds of these two cities left a profound mark on the "sound" and "style" born in each place.
        </p>

        <p>
          What exactly defines "Mentai Rock"? Within Fukuoka Prefecture itself, it remains a subject of lively debate.
        </p>

        <p>
          Generally, the rock music that emerged from Fukuoka Prefecture between the 1970s and early 1980s is referred to as "Mentai Rock." Most people regard Sonhouse—formed in Fukuoka in the 1970s by Toshiyuki Shibayama, Makoto Ayukawa, and others—as the true fountainhead of the movement.
        </p>

        <p>
          Following Sonhouse's major-label debut in 1975, a wave of rock bands from Fukuoka broke into the major scene one after another: Sheena & The Rokkets (debuted in 1978), ARB (1978), The Rockers (1980), The Roosters (1980), and THE MODS (1981).
        </p>

        <p>
          A widely held theory suggests that the term "Mentai Rock" itself was coined around the time of The Roosters' debut. Because of that, which specific bands qualify as "Mentai Rock bands" is sometimes debated.
        </p>

        <p>
          Across Japan as a whole, however, many people simply use "Mentai Rock" as a collective name for the rock scene of Fukuoka.
        </p>

        <p class="signature">— <a href={`${base}en/rock/voices/#dobb`}>Dobb</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <a class="pillars-band" href={`${base}en/rock/two-pillars/`}>
      <img src={`${base}images/rock/pillars-hero.jpg`} alt={`Makoto Ayukawa holding his Gibson Les Paul backstage beside a Sheena & The Rokkets T-shirt. Photo by FU.`} loading="lazy" />
      <div class="pb-text">
        <p class="pb-title">The Two Pillars</p>
        <p class="pb-sub">Sheena &amp; The Rokkets and The Roosters</p>
      </div>
    </a>

    <section class="industrial">
      <div class="intro-inner">
        <hr class="navy-rule" />
        <h2 class="section-q">The Industrial Blues</h2>
        <p class="ind-sub">Detroit and Northern Kyushu</p>
        <p class="sub-q"><em>Why Raw Rock 'n' Roll Resonated Through the Smoke and Steel of Kitakyushu</em></p>
        <h3 class="ind-h3">Comparative Architecture: Heavy Industry & Global Music Roots</h3>
        <div class="ind-table-wrap">
          <table class="ind-table">
            <thead><tr><th>City / Region</th><th>Industrial Engine</th><th>Musical Genesis</th><th>Rhythmic Identity & Soil</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>Detroit</strong> <em>(USA / Midwest)</em></td><td>Automotive Manufacturing (Ford, GM, Chrysler)</td><td>Motown <em>(Motor Town)</em> & Proto-Punk <em>(MC5, The Stooges)</em></td><td>Assembly line precision meets spiritual soul; driving, unyielding backbeats shaped within an industrial metropolis.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Chicago</strong> <em>(USA / Midwest)</em></td><td>Meatpacking, Steel, Rail Hub (Destination of the Great Migration)</td><td>Chicago Blues <em>(Chess Records)</em></td><td>Rural Delta acoustic roots amplified into electric defiance; heavy, dragging shuffles echoing urban and industrial weight.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Birmingham</strong> <em>(UK / Midlands)</em></td><td>Steel, Foundries, Heavy Metalwork (Heart of the Industrial Revolution)</td><td>Heavy Metal <em>(Black Sabbath)</em></td><td>Metallic, hammering guitar riffs emerging from a working-class foundry city; dark, heavy grooves.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Manchester</strong> <em>(UK / North)</em></td><td>Cotton Mills, Warehouses, Post-Industrial Decline</td><td>Post-Punk <em>(Joy Division)</em> & Madchester</td><td>Stark, urgent rhythms and cavernous soundscapes born out of a transforming post-industrial cityscape.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Northern Kyushu</strong> <em>(Japan / Kanmon & Chikuho)</em></td><td>Yahata Steel Works, Coal Mining (Birthplace of Modern Japanese Industry)</td><td>Mentai Rock <em>(Regional Blues / Garage Rock)</em></td><td>A stripped-down blend of blues, rhythm & blues, rock 'n' roll, and garage rock associated with the regional scene.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <h3 class="ind-h3">1. The Universal Rhythm of Smoke and Steel</h3>
        <p>
          True roots music rarely begins in polished salons. It grows out of factory towns, railway corridors, and shipping docks—places where heavy industry, migration, clubs, and record shops shape how musicians meet and create. Across the 20th century, the physical environment of heavy labor repeatedly gave rise to raw musical movements, from Detroit’s automotive plants to the industrial cities of Britain.
        </p>
        <h3 class="ind-h3">2. Yahata: The Industrial Epicenter of Modern Japan</h3>
        <p>
          Established in 1901, the state-run Yahata Steel Works helped transform northern Kyushu into one of modern Japan’s major industrial regions, connected to the coalfields of Chikuho and the maritime routes of Kanmon. Beneath the smoke, steel, and manual labor, a distinct local psyche emerged—stoic, defiant, and physically grounded.
        </p>
        <h3 class="ind-h3">3. Defining "Mentai Rock": The Regional Counter-Current</h3>
        <p>
          While Japan’s commercial music industry was heavily concentrated in Tokyo during the 1970s and 1980s, Fukuoka and Northern Kyushu developed a distinct regional current that later came to be associated with “Mentai Rock.” Bands such as Sonhouse, Sheena & The Rokkets, and The Roosters drew on blues, rhythm and blues, rock ’n’ roll, punk, and garage rock, transforming those influences through the urgency of their own regional scene.
        </p>
        <h3 class="ind-h3">4. The Eyewitness Perspective: A Circle Completed</h3>
        <p>
          Having encountered this music firsthand, and later living within the musical worlds of New York and Havana, Seina looks back across the Pacific and the Caribbean to read Mentai Rock not as a simple imitation of Western rock, but as a regional transformation shaped by Northern Kyushu.
        </p>
        <p class="signature">— G・Vega (AI, Gemini)</p>
      </div>
    </section>



    <hr class="section-divider" />

    <section class="war-block">
      <h2 class="war-title">Voices of the Scene</h2>
      <div class="kw-grid">
        {koreanWar.map((c) => (
          <a class="kw-card" href={c.href}>
            <div class="kw-image"><img src={c.image} alt={c.alt} loading="lazy" /></div>
            <h3 class="kw-cardtitle">{c.title}</h3>
            <p class="kw-desc">{c.desc}</p>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="section-divider" />

    <p class="photo-credit">Photos courtesy of <a href="https://www.fuweb.co.jp/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FU</a></p>
  </main>
  <Footer lang="en" />
</Layout>

<style>
